UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 15, Lesson 1: Performing for an audience', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"When you perform for an audience, you need to be heard and seen. Projection means speaking clearly and loudly enough for the back of the room without shouting. Face the audience, or at least turn your body partly out, so people can see your face. Pause between lines so the audience can react. Good audience members sit quietly, watch carefully, laugh in the right places and clap at the end. After the performance, performers bow together. It is normal to be nervous, and slow breaths can help.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA performer speaks too quietly and faces the back wall. Write two changes that would help the audience, and one thing the audience should do.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Turn to face the audience and speak louder from your belly, taking a slow breath first. The audience should listen quietly and clap at the end.'' Facing out and projection make performers easier to hear, and a kind audience supports them.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that a loud voice means shouting. Projection means a clear, strong voice from a relaxed body, and shouting can strain your throat and make the words unclear.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPerform a one-minute scene for family members. Before you start, write three goals such as facing out, projecting and pausing. Afterwards, ask for two stars and a wish and record which goal you met. Practise slow breathing if you feel nervous.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"When you perform for an audience, you need to be heard and seen. Projection means speaking clearly and loudly enough for the back of the room without shouting. Face the audience, or at least turn your body partly out, so people can see your face. Pause between lines so the audience can react. Good audience members sit quietly, watch carefully, laugh in the right places and clap at the end. After the performance, performers bow together. It is normal to be nervous, and slow breaths can help.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-DRA-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Perform a one-minute scene for family members. Before you start, write three goals such as facing out, projecting and pausing. Afterwards, ask for two stars and a wish and record which goal you met. Practise slow breathi Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Turn to face the audience and speak louder from your belly, taking a slow breath first. The audience should listen quietly and clap at the end.'' Facing out and projection make… Reasoning: I used an audience by attending to projection rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking that a loud voice means shouting. Projection means a clear, strong voice from a relaxed body, and shouting can strain your throat and make the words… Correction: Strong answer: ''Turn to face the audience and speak louder from your belly, taking a slow breath first. The audience should listen quietly and clap at the… Why: the correction restores accurate use of an audience.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that a loud voice means shouting. Projection means a clear, strong voice from a relaxed body, and shouting can strain your throat and make the words unclear. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating an audience on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with an audience, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Turn to face the audience and speak louder from your belly, taking a slow breath first. The audience should listen quietly and clap at the… This matches the lesson skill for an audience.","options":["Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''Turn to face the audience and speak louder from your belly, taking a slow breath first. The audience should listen quietly and clap at the…","Thinking that a loud voice means shouting. Projection means a clear, strong voice from a relaxed body, and shouting can strain your throat and make the words…","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"A performer speaks too quietly and faces the back wall. Write two changes that would help the audience, and one thing the audience should do. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"expla