new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Thinking that a loud voice means shouting. Projection means a clear, strong voice from a relaxed body, and shouting can strain your throat and make the words…","Perform a one-minute scene for family members. Before you start, write three goals such as facing out, projecting and pausing. Afterwards, ask for…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of an audience?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating an audience on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Turn to face the audience and speak louder from your belly, taking a slow breath first. The audience should listen quietly and clap at the end.'' Facing out and projection make… Reasoning: I used an audience by attending to projection rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with an audience, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking that a loud voice means shouting. Projection means a clear, strong voice from a relaxed body, and shouting can strain your throat and make the words… Correction: Strong answer: ''Turn to face the audience and speak louder from your belly, taking a slow breath first. The audience should listen quietly and clap at the… Why: the correction restores accurate use of an audience.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w15-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise an audience.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Drama","success_criteria":["I can use an audience with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify projection and explain why it matters.","I can practise facing out independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a bow needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"When you perform for an audience, you need to be heard and seen. Projection means speaking clearly and loudly enough for the back of the room without shouting. Face the audience, or at least turn your body partly out, so people can see your face. Pause between lines so the…","options":["When you perform for an audience, you need to be heard and seen. Projection means speaking clearly and loudly enough for the back of the room without shouting. Face the audience, or at least turn your body partly out, so people can see your face. Pause between lines so the audience can react. Good audience members sit quietly, watch carefully, laugh in the right places and clap at the end. After the performance, performers bow together. It is normal to be nervous, and slow breaths can help.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about an audience?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Tessa speaks her line, ''The treasure is buried here!'', using a clear voice that reaches the back row, with her body angled toward the audience. She waits for a moment of silence before the next line. The audience laughs when the pirate trips and then sits quietly during the tense part. At the end, all actors bow together.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA performer speaks too quietly and faces the back wall. Write two changes that would help the audience, and one thing the audience should do.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Turn to face the audience and speak louder from your belly, taking a slow breath first. The audience should listen quietly and clap at the end.'' Facing out and projection make performers easier to hear, and a kind audience supports them.","explain":"When you perform for an audience, you need to be heard and seen. Projection means speaking clearly and loudly enough for the back of the room without shouting. Face the audience, or at least turn your body partly out, so people can see your face. Pause between lines so the audience can react. Good audience members sit quietly, watch carefully, laugh in the right places and clap at the end. After the performance, performers bow together. It is normal to be nervous, and slow breaths can help.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that a loud voice means shouting. Projection means a clear, strong voice from a relaxed body, and shouting can strain your throat and make the words unclear. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Projection and facing out.","caption":"Be heard and seen.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Project</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">clear voice to the back</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Face out</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">body turned to audience</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Tessa speaks her line, ''The treasure is buried here!'', using a clear voice that reaches the back row, with her  Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Tessa speaks her line, ''The treasure is buried here!'', using a clear voice that reaches the back row, with her  Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique 